  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Building a White Nation shows early signs of use and reach: it has been downloaded 1,305 times through OAPEN, and the record also shows institutional support from KU Leuven and Freie Universiteit Berlin. At the same time, the scholarly citation count is still at zero, so the book\u2019s longer-term research uptake is not yet established in the citation record.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Katharina J\u00f6rder\u2019s <em>Building a White Nation</em> examines the apartheid-era South African state\u2019s use of photography as part of its propaganda machinery, with a focus on how visual culture was mobilized to promote Afrikaner nationalism and reinforce white rule. The book has already found measurable readership: OAPEN records 1,305 downloads, indicating active use beyond its publication moment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The support record adds another sign of institutional recognition. Funding is associated with KU Leuven and Freie Universiteit Berlin, which suggests the project was developed within a scholarly environment that helped sustain the research and its dissemination. The book is also available in open access form through the publisher record, which likely supports its visibility and reuse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are comparatively modest at this stage. OpenAlex currently reports zero citations, so there is no citation-based evidence yet of downstream scholarly uptake. That should be read as an early-stage record rather than a judgment on significance: for a 2023 book, citation accumulation may still be developing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding metadata points to a work positioned at the intersection of history, visual culture, politics, and media studies, with attention to the institutional context of photographic propaganda. Taken together, the available evidence shows a book that is already being accessed and supported, while its longer-term research influence remains to be demonstrated through future citations and related scholarly engagement.</p>",
